---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---

<Base
  title="Careers"
  description="Careers at Burn and Churn Coffee, a fictional café. No real jobs are offered."
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <span class="eyebrow">Careers</span>
    <h1>Work behind the counter</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Burn and Churn Coffee is a fictional business, so there are <strong>no real jobs</strong> here
      and no applications are accepted. This page describes the team the story is built around.
    </p>

    <h2>The team</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Role</th>
          <th scope="col">What they do</th>
          <th scope="col">How many</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Barista</td>
          <td>Pulls shots, steams milk, takes orders at the counter, keeps the bar clean</td>
          <td>15</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shift lead</td>
          <td>Runs a shift: the order board, sold-out items, opening and closing checks</td>
          <td>3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Store manager</td>
          <td>The menu, schedules, refunds and the day-to-day running of the shop</td>
          <td>1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Owner</td>
          <td>Runs the business and signs off on its security program</td>
          <td>1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>IT technician</td>
          <td>Part time: accounts, devices and the systems behind the counter</td>
          <td>1</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each role gets exactly the access it needs, no more. Staff sign in to the staff portal with
      their work account, and their role comes with them. As the register and payments arrive, the
      portal checks that role on every action: for example, a barista will be able to take a payment
      but not refund one.
    </p>

    <h2>Applying</h2>
    <p>
      There's nothing to apply for, and this site doesn't collect applications or CVs. If you're
      interested in the project itself, read <a href="/about/">our story</a> or{' '}
      <a href="/security/">how the site is built</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
